review_text:
squad pinned down by an enemy m3 quad;
selected one with a panzerfaust hiding nearby;
direct-controlled him in 1st person mode to slip through houses, neutralizing two opponent soldiers on the way;
finally made it to the rear of the m3, and selected panzerfaust, ready to punch a hole in it;
accidentally exited direct control mode (pressed E accidentally) and he returned to AI;
he pondered for a second and launched the panzerfaust on a near by jeep;
the quad turned around and wasted him;
the squad pinned down was no more.
10 out of 10.

You will need a lot of patience to play this game, since it has: 
- 1 - Bad AI: You can have dozens of individual soldiers in a match, but you need to manually do almost everything for them, like throwing grenades, using AT, and path finding. If the soldier is inside a building or next to big objects, it is almost guaranteed that he will mess up anything you command him to do, especially if it is using explosives. Having to manually control your soldiers for basic things makes this game more of a FPS than a RTS, since you will spend more time controlling soldiers individually than controlling your army as a whole and making strategic maneuvers; 
- 2 - Multiplayer LAG: This game is very hard to play in the multiplayer if you or your enemy have a ping higher than 100ms. The game lags a LOT and also it seems like your soldiers will react a lot slower to the enemy in situations like these. Adding this with the fact that the AI is already not great, you will really need a LOT of patience to play PVP here, since a lot of things will not go as you planned. The game really needs a lot more optimization; 
- 3 - Questionable decisions of development: I play this game almost since it has been launched (actually I played the original Call to Arms Beta back in 2016), and these aforementioned problems exist since its origin, and I did not notice a lot of improvement in the AI and the LAG over the years. I have seen a lot of new content being added in the game, but the main problems still persists. Why focus on bringing new content and not focus on fixing the main problems (AI, LAG, optimization)? It does not matter if there is new content in the game, because if the gameplay is broken, it will still be broken with the new content...; 
I think the game really has potential, but I have not seen decent improvement throughout the years (even though the game has frequent updates), so I cannot recommend it. If the game was still in its Beta version, these things would be more acceptable, but for a launched game, it just doesn't work as it should.

Probably the best WW2 RTS with incredible detail & non-boring simulation:
Pick guns, manage inventory of every soldier, give Your medic a machine gun or change his helmet, roast enemy tank with molotov cocktails, refill empty fuel tank of Your tank, complete mission with single soldier...
If there's a thing You would like to do in war simulation - You probably can do it in this game.

Ever wanted to feel what it's like to knock out an enemy tank, repair & re-crew it using Your infantry and use that same tank against enemy - this is the game.

My favorite time-killer sandbox style:
1. Choose campaign;
2. Set up Your defences;
3. Smash enemy reinforcements;
4. Scavenge what's left;
5. Finish mission;
6. Go to the next one.

There are alot of great reviews and youtube reviews already around that can detail what makes this game so amazing already. so i'll just keep mine simple =)

This game is fantastic. Its like a realistic CoH with some War Thunder dashed in. A great depth of micro-managment if you're into that. The games realistic nature put you on your toes a bit keeping units supplied with ammo, fuel and equipment, but that will quickly become second nature =)

/// EDIT \\\ 
When I wrote this review originally at only 90 hours of gameplay, I mentioned that my only downside of this game was that is only focused on the Eastern Front. but in 2024 they released easily the best DLC to date (Liberation) which adds the western front. this pretty much doubled the games size and with that I personally have nothing but praise for this game. 
/// END EDIT \\\

This game has quickly become one of my favourate WW2 games...once I got past that learning curve ^^;
A beautiful, immersive and realistic game that portrays WW2 in a way most games never have done before.

I consider myself to be a big fun of Brothers in Arms title, but I can NOT recommend "Hell's Highway".

Yes, that an old game, but I'm not judging based on modern standards. This game is still bad when comparing with its predecessors - "Road to Hill 30" and "Earned in Blood". If you are new to Brothers in Arms title - go and check out "Road to Hill 30" and "Earned in Blood". If you have already seen previous games, then prepare yourself for disappointment.

I've made up some sort of comparison of "Hell's Highway" with previous games:

I'd better start with cons:
 * bad gameplay. There is no more authentic feel like previous game had. This game feels like a game for kids ("Medal of Honor" or smth like that). Health is quickly regenerated. Many covers do not work if you are not 'leaning' (i.e. simply crouching behind cover).
 * bad storytelling. Really. It's awkwardly awful;
 * bad graphics. I don't know why, but graphics from previous games was more appealing;
 * bad audio. Usually I don't really pay attention to this aspect, but audio effect in this game are a bit irritating and unrealistic;

Pros:
 * there are missions where you can drive a tank. This has nothing to do with realism, but still could be nice;
 * environment is a bit more destructible;
 * teams got some special weapons - MG & bazooka.

Brothers In Arms: Hell's Highway
pros:
//fast game with modern day graphics with easy controls that moves from location A to B to C and so on
//good amount of action and special effects
cons:
//some difficult battles
//some lack of directions/instructions

//good game
Personal Suggested Purchase Price: $1.49 Or Less During Sale

This game i tried i really tried so i'll be brief:

Good:
- Excellent costumization;
- Well layed out world that offers you different tactical advantages;
- Engaging firefights along with continuous stealth oportunities.

Bad:
- Broken A.I. (both team and enemies);
- Uninteresting world and villains;
- Ubisoft patented bad open world design (from NPC's, to wildlife, to content);
- Extremely lazy, crazy boring and repetitive side quests (another trademark of Ubisoft);
- Bad leveling system (mostly unimpressive and not needed as some of the rewards should come from the start);
- A world that somehow feels void of life.

Get it at a discount, perhabs you'll love it and have a different opinion, but be prepared for how miserable uninteresting it is. I love the genre of games, especially GR and i did my effort to push through it but it was just plain broken and boring.

I can't say it's a perfect game. It does suffer from some Ubisoft cliches. But hear me out.

If you just:
- set difficulty to maximum possible;
- disable most of HUD elements - especially minimap, item and enemy markers;
- do not allocate skill points into skills that are obviously making game much easier - like more HP, rebel HP, supressor without damage penalty etc;
- limit (not necessarily completely) use of drone - use binoculars instead where possible;
- limit use of sync shot - only use when necessary;
Then you get possibly the best open world tactical shooter currently available. Especially fun with friends.

Can recommend on sale, on which it goes pretty often.

GRW is yet another example of the need for a neutral review option. The first half of the game is great—gorgeous visuals, an active open world, gunplay with a good balance between realism and arcade, tons of moddable weapons, a serviceable plot that drives some fun action sequences.

Story... You play as Nomad, a Ghost Recon operative sent to Bolivia with your team to commando your way through the hierarchy of a massive drug cartel, with the end goal of eliminating the kingpin. It's essentially preposterous but manages to keep all the open world chaos focused.

The problem is that everything the game does well devolves into a grindy slog by the mid to late game. Ubisoft tried to keep the missions varied, with several resolving in ways other than shoot->kill, but it's effort that ultimately rings hollow. It's a Ubisoft game after all, which means icons strewn all over the map, and the overwhelming majority of your time can be spent collecting weapons and upgrades if that's your thing. Ideally for Ubisoft, however, you'll get so fed up that you'll visit the in-game store and spend real money. Indeed, pretty much everything [visually] cool is locked inside loot boxes, and the sheer dedication required to collect every gun, mod and upgrade is significant ... probably tens or dozens of hours.

It's not just the grind and microtransactions that ruined the experience, though. The second half of the game is riddled with bugs. It's so buggy, even Bethesda would be ashamed.

Let us count the ways:

- enemies spawning behind or on top of me and my co-op partner (not wandering into us, but actually spawning out of the ether while we watched);
- "follow without being detected" objectives having targets warp through walls;
- mission icons failing to show up;
- mission critical NPCs making progress impossible by refusing to move;
- vehicles bugging out and not letting you stow high-value targets, or not letting you retrieve them once they've been stowed (had a target get blown up in a car because I couldn't take him out of the backseat);
- desync errors in co-op games that have NPC targets in different places for different players;
- general annoyances like important dialogue failing to play, or in some cases playing way too often.

All of this stuff happened repeatedly, and I nearly uninstalled several times before finally finishing the campaign. Forget all the explosive gunfights and car chases—the most difficult end-game challenge in GRW is getting around the bugs. In retrospect, it probably wasn't worth it, but there were enough good moments that I don't feel completely bad about it.

The two DLC campaigns (Narco Road and Fallen Ghosts) are trash. The former shoe-horns monster trucks and extreme sports into the game with the excuse of getting in good with some narcos to take out a boss. It's a terrible idea, terribly executed. The latter introduces an overabundance of bullet sponge enemies that feel more like something out The Division rather than GRW. Mind you, I enjoyed The Division, but that's not what I want out of Ghost Recon, and I just didn't care anymore after a few missions. Neither DLC is worth the money, and I do regret getting the Gold Edition now.

PvP modes are lackluster and mostly dead. Don't bother.

Guerilla mode (essentially a horde mode) is okay, but difficult and grindy for two people. Four human players might have better luck, but who cares?

If you can snag the base game on sale, and you like semi-realistic-but-mostly-arcade military gameplay, Ghost Recon Wildlands might be worth a go. It is not worth full price, and the DLCs aren't worth any price. If I knew before I bought it what I know now, I might have spent the money on something else.

